2
00:00:28,835 --> 00:00:34,018
[SPEAKER_00]: You can feel the tendency there, or the pressure there, but you don't...

3
00:00:36,420 --> 00:00:45,587
[SPEAKER_00]: But then the mind goes into that, just investigating, just naturally, like, okay, I got a red flag.

4
00:00:47,402 --> 00:00:48,703
[SPEAKER_00]: This is unskillful.

5
00:00:49,663 --> 00:00:50,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it unskillful?

6
00:00:51,324 --> 00:00:52,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Then the dominoes start falling.

7
00:00:52,985 --> 00:00:53,685
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it unskillful?

8
00:00:53,745 --> 00:00:56,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Because in that cloud, you're not getting wisdom.

9
00:00:57,107 --> 00:00:58,628
[SPEAKER_00]: This is not peaceful.

10
00:00:58,708 --> 00:00:59,488
[SPEAKER_00]: It's agitated.

11
00:01:00,369 --> 00:01:01,009
[SPEAKER_00]: This is a problem.

12
00:01:03,510 --> 00:01:05,691
[SPEAKER_00]: In a way, it's just, you know, what is it based on?

13
00:01:05,912 --> 00:01:07,192
[SPEAKER_00]: The body.

14
00:01:08,073 --> 00:01:08,993
[SPEAKER_00]: That body over there.

15
00:01:09,133 --> 00:01:09,734
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not mine.

16
00:01:11,114 --> 00:01:13,616
[SPEAKER_00]: It's in the situation, you know, with that view of that situation.

17
00:01:14,056 --> 00:01:15,537
[SPEAKER_01]: While it's, you know,

18
00:01:18,810 --> 00:01:26,213
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like, eh... if lust comes out, there is automatically a problem.

19
00:01:27,874 --> 00:01:29,014
[SPEAKER_00]: No, because you're not free from it.

20
00:01:29,074 --> 00:01:30,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

21
00:01:30,755 --> 00:01:36,738
[SPEAKER_01]: So, because you're not free from it, you have to be doing all that you just described.

22
00:01:39,439 --> 00:01:41,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're not free, eh...

23
00:01:43,613 --> 00:01:51,376
[SPEAKER_01]: From the nature of lust, because you're still subject, or you're still liable to lust, means all those things.

24
00:01:51,776 --> 00:01:57,979
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, it's sort of going in the right direction, but it's still not there, because...

25
00:01:58,279 --> 00:02:04,882
[SPEAKER_01]: When I say going in the right direction, I mean, yeah, so you're trying to understand the nature of lust, so to prevent the future arising.

26
00:02:07,223 --> 00:02:07,943
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as I said,

27
00:02:09,418 --> 00:02:17,438
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is much deeper than just one particular arising of one particular lust or anger or whatever.

28
00:02:17,979 --> 00:02:18,681
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is that

29
00:02:19,805 --> 00:02:20,865
[SPEAKER_01]: It is able to arise.

30
00:02:21,305 --> 00:02:29,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, lust remains possible, and you are not free from the possibility of your mind being infected by lust, or by aversion, or by whatever.

31
00:02:29,607 --> 00:02:31,108
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what you need to strive at.

32
00:02:31,608 --> 00:02:41,190
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when the lust arises, if there is a pull, or if the anger arises, if there is an aversion, or whatever you want to call it, means that's a problem.

33
00:02:43,218 --> 00:02:44,319
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's already a problem.

34
00:02:44,639 --> 00:02:49,862
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that that possibility of anger, and now the actual anger, affects you.

35
00:02:50,723 --> 00:02:56,426
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever you do then, will be on account of that lust, will be on account of that anger.

36
00:02:57,827 --> 00:03:01,650
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the most... that's already not good, fundamentally speaking.

37
00:03:02,270 --> 00:03:09,475
[SPEAKER_01]: But, it's still within that of... it's not ideal, since you cannot but not do anything,

38
00:03:10,499 --> 00:03:14,020
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're not free from the... the nature of it.

39
00:03:14,260 --> 00:03:18,982
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not free from the nature of lust, so you will end up... whatever you do will be because of the reason of lust.

40
00:03:19,802 --> 00:03:29,546
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, the best... and that's what you can do, is aim that whatever you have no choice but to do, is aimed at undoing the nature of the lust.

41
00:03:31,407 --> 00:03:34,668
[SPEAKER_01]: So, restraint, reflection that undermines the nature of lust and so on.

42
00:03:35,720 --> 00:03:41,482
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, that's a form of response, which means... and if you need to respond, it means you're affected.

43
00:03:44,542 --> 00:03:53,185
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as I said, for a Patujan, or in the case of a Krishna view of, oh, that's not a snake, that's a rope.

44
00:03:54,305 --> 00:03:56,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you have to go and find out it's a rope.

45
00:03:56,246 --> 00:03:57,626
[SPEAKER_01]: That's completely misdirected.

46
00:03:58,026 --> 00:04:00,327
[SPEAKER_01]: That's directed outside, projected onto the thing.

47
00:04:01,007 --> 00:04:04,408
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if... and that is a natural tendency.

48
00:04:04,448 --> 00:04:05,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if there is a lustful

49
00:04:06,192 --> 00:04:17,817
[SPEAKER_01]: object causing, like, infecting your mind with lust, you try and find the fault in the object, or find the fault in the object that caused you the anger and stuff, and that's already misplaced.

50
00:04:19,557 --> 00:04:30,662
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to be able to accept it, accept responsibility for it, means withstand that arising for as long as it wants to arise, which I wait it wants to arise, and then

51
00:04:32,312 --> 00:04:47,062
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to think carefully, and wisely, basically, make that whatever you do, won't be misdirected at the particular thing, or internally, or externally, or both, or neither.

52
00:04:47,082 --> 00:04:52,205
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be thinking about, like, feeling bad about yourself.

53
00:04:52,745 --> 00:04:54,186
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I made a mistake, whatever.

54
00:04:54,527 --> 00:04:58,389
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't... or hating on the object, or hating on yourself, whatever.

55
00:04:58,849 --> 00:04:59,570
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to do,

56
00:05:01,850 --> 00:05:10,737
[SPEAKER_01]: A type of reflection that will end at that possibility of lust, of aversion, of delusion, of the fact that you are still subject to it.

57
00:05:12,158 --> 00:05:20,224
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether the present arising just reminded you of that, that you're still subject to it, but when you start dealing with it, you wanna

58
00:05:21,405 --> 00:05:32,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Develop your efforts, so to speak, that they transcend a particular concern about this lustful object or that hateful object, but start dealing with the nature of lust and nature of hate.

59
00:05:33,510 --> 00:05:43,153
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's extra hard in the beginning because the mind is pulled by the object and the problem means the object, but actually it's not.

60
00:05:43,313 --> 00:05:46,815
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why delusion is a habit that's been cultivated for so long.

61
00:05:47,598 --> 00:05:54,743
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, the assumption that what has arisen now, what bothers me, whether by causing lust or aversion, that's the problem.

62
00:05:54,923 --> 00:05:55,663
[SPEAKER_01]: I need to deal with it.

63
00:05:56,004 --> 00:05:59,626
[SPEAKER_01]: And you deal with it and you feel immediate satisfaction because you dealt with it, one way or the other.

64
00:05:59,646 --> 00:06:01,147
[SPEAKER_01]: You gave in or overcome.

65
00:06:01,928 --> 00:06:03,068
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's still misplaced.

66
00:06:05,770 --> 00:06:10,113
[SPEAKER_01]: But the more you look at it like this, the more that becomes obvious.

67
00:06:10,133 --> 00:06:16,758
[SPEAKER_01]: That actually, yeah, I might be able to deal with this particular problem, with this particular arising, with that particular thing that I've just perceived.

68
00:06:17,525 --> 00:06:18,345
[SPEAKER_01]: But what if I can't?

69
00:06:20,586 --> 00:06:27,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, more likely, and everybody experienced that, yeah, I can deal with it, and I dealt with it, and then what?

70
00:06:27,549 --> 00:06:28,930
[SPEAKER_00]: Until the next one comes along.

71
00:06:30,150 --> 00:06:33,572
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the next one, and then the next one, and then the next one.

72
00:06:33,592 --> 00:06:35,653
[SPEAKER_00]: I know, yeah, I know when I deal with something like that.

73
00:06:36,313 --> 00:06:38,114
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't deal with it completely.

74
00:06:38,554 --> 00:06:40,295
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, oh, okay, I've only done so much.

75
00:06:40,475 --> 00:06:43,256
[SPEAKER_01]: So that type of escape, and then it's not really an escape.

76
00:06:45,884 --> 00:06:47,785
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to have to do anything.

77
00:06:48,226 --> 00:06:57,633
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as I said, in that... in that Hindu... Krishna example of, oh, you have fear arising on account of some... on account of a snake that you've just seen.

78
00:06:58,634 --> 00:06:59,014
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

79
00:07:00,895 --> 00:07:05,399
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, they say, ah, but then you look closer and then turns out it's a rope.

80
00:07:06,079 --> 00:07:07,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Therefore, your fear was not real.

81
00:07:08,741 --> 00:07:10,222
[SPEAKER_01]: That's completely misdirected.

82
00:07:11,783 --> 00:07:13,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, what if you can't look closer?

83
00:07:14,933 --> 00:07:19,774
[SPEAKER_01]: But what if you look even closer and you find out it's a snake, that looks like a roe?

84
00:07:21,434 --> 00:07:30,196
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to have to do anything on account of thing, because that means, whether it's good or bad, you're affected by that thing.

85
00:07:30,296 --> 00:07:39,897
[SPEAKER_01]: If you need to respond to it, if you need to find out that that roe, that that snake is a roe, means you were affected by the nature of the arising of a thing.

86
00:07:41,338 --> 00:07:43,698
[SPEAKER_01]: So, once you start thinking like that, whether you see a snake,

87
00:07:44,683 --> 00:07:45,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Or a rope.

88
00:07:46,265 --> 00:07:46,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter.

89
00:07:48,329 --> 00:07:50,632
[SPEAKER_01]: You see a thing, fear arose.

90
00:07:51,173 --> 00:07:52,695
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the problem, that's what you deal with it.

91
00:07:53,236 --> 00:07:58,424
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're not concerned to go and look closer to see if it's a rope, because, ah, yeah, good, then my fear is not real.

92
00:07:59,753 --> 00:08:07,959
[SPEAKER_01]: That particular fear turned out to be unfounded, but finding out that it's unfounded, it's a different experience from the original fear.

93
00:08:08,419 --> 00:08:14,504
[SPEAKER_01]: So even, even if it was the actual rope that you thought it was a snake, it was a real experience as such.

94
00:08:15,665 --> 00:08:21,749
[SPEAKER_01]: It was experience of something I think is a snake that turns out actually to be a rope, that it's frightening me.

95
00:08:25,612 --> 00:08:28,855
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why... well, that's why that doesn't work.

96
00:08:30,003 --> 00:08:33,284
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why psychology doesn't work either, in terms of freedom from suffering.

97
00:08:33,944 --> 00:08:40,846
[SPEAKER_01]: It always required this management response in regard to things that arise and bother you.

98
00:08:42,286 --> 00:08:44,726
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh... again, you just stop and think about it.

99
00:08:44,746 --> 00:08:49,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I might be able to deal with this, but I might... like, now I am able to walk and find out that it's a road.

100
00:08:50,308 --> 00:08:51,248
[SPEAKER_01]: But what if I can't do it?

101
00:08:52,491 --> 00:08:57,454
[SPEAKER_01]: Then I'll remain affected by this fear that might be unfounded or not.

102
00:08:57,594 --> 00:08:59,596
[SPEAKER_01]: But either way, it is real as such.

103
00:09:00,056 --> 00:09:01,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can say, oh, it's not real.

104
00:09:01,437 --> 00:09:04,379
[SPEAKER_01]: That fear is not real because that might be a rope.

105
00:09:04,659 --> 00:09:05,520
[SPEAKER_01]: You can say even that.

106
00:09:05,560 --> 00:09:10,723
[SPEAKER_01]: But even then, in that case, the experience of unreal fear is still frightening.

107
00:09:11,424 --> 00:09:20,870
[SPEAKER_01]: So whether you label it real or unreal or both or neither, the fact that on the level of feelings there is suffering, that's the problem.

108
00:09:22,613 --> 00:09:29,181
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it requires a magical belief that unreal frees me from suffering.

109
00:09:29,221 --> 00:09:30,342
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no really connection.

110
00:09:30,362 --> 00:09:33,265
[SPEAKER_01]: The only connection between the two is the ignorance.

111
00:09:34,547 --> 00:09:37,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Not knowing that actually your suffering remains unaffected.

112
00:09:54,795 --> 00:09:55,435
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how it works.

113
00:09:55,675 --> 00:09:57,296
[SPEAKER_01]: You see a something, a snake.

114
00:09:58,396 --> 00:10:04,317
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever arises, there is always an implicit possibility of it being otherwise.

115
00:10:07,498 --> 00:10:08,739
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's just the nature of things.

116
00:10:09,319 --> 00:10:12,519
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the same way, oh, I saw a snake, Fireroes.

117
00:10:13,240 --> 00:10:14,620
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't misplace the problem,

118
00:10:15,576 --> 00:10:39,016
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't project it out onto that thing that you just perceived, if you just take it as a reminder that you can still be frightened, means you're not free from the nature of fear, regardless of any particular manifestation of it, an object that manifests it, then, if you find out, one way or the other, that it's a rope, then that experience of the rope is the experience of the rope that I thought it was that snake.

119
00:10:41,078 --> 00:10:43,279
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is a relief on the particular level,

120
00:10:44,021 --> 00:10:49,945
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you look at it as a nature thing, it means, ah, next time, it might actually be a snake.

121
00:10:49,985 --> 00:10:54,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Next time, I might see a rope, that I think it's a rope and I'm not afraid.

122
00:10:54,707 --> 00:10:56,809
[SPEAKER_01]: Then I come closer and I realize it's a snake.

123
00:10:57,609 --> 00:11:04,493
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how does then, like this, this interpretation of Krishna stack up, saying, ah, well, see, it was a rope.

124
00:11:05,054 --> 00:11:08,556
[SPEAKER_01]: Therefore, your fear and your whole experience is an illusion.

125
00:11:08,716 --> 00:11:09,937
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing to be afraid of.

126
00:11:10,357 --> 00:11:11,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what if it's the other way around?

127
00:11:12,452 --> 00:11:16,975
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you think it's a rope, but then it's a snake, because there are snakes in the world, then what?

128
00:11:17,975 --> 00:11:18,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you...

129
00:11:18,516 --> 00:11:18,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Hold on.

130
00:11:19,356 --> 00:11:24,699
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, this illusion, you can still call it an illusion, but that doesn't reduce your fear in any way.

131
00:11:26,620 --> 00:11:35,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can say, oh, crap, the experience of non-threatening things can be threatening if I find out closer.

132
00:11:36,066 --> 00:11:37,266
[SPEAKER_01]: So you reverse completely.

133
00:11:37,787 --> 00:11:40,068
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's why you need a belief and blind belief.

134
00:11:40,498 --> 00:11:43,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereby you only look selectively at the things that suit you.

135
00:11:44,640 --> 00:11:50,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Because in reality, actually, the latter belief is more close to the nature of things.

136
00:11:50,903 --> 00:11:53,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereby you think that everything can be a snake.

137
00:11:54,625 --> 00:11:54,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

138
00:11:54,966 --> 00:12:03,890
[SPEAKER_01]: Because an arising of things, in general, upon which your existence depends and is defined by, will cease eventually.

139
00:12:04,190 --> 00:12:04,451
[SPEAKER_01]: Death.

140
00:12:06,512 --> 00:12:09,173
[SPEAKER_01]: So rather than like, oh no, it's all illusion, I'm fine.

141
00:12:10,269 --> 00:12:21,356
[SPEAKER_01]: You can undo it and say, well, even the pleasure that I'm experiencing here, or even the peace that I'm experiencing here, in that sense, you can say it's illusory, because it will come to an end.

142
00:12:26,219 --> 00:12:28,341
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you realize that's not the illusion they talk about.

143
00:12:30,342 --> 00:12:37,647
[SPEAKER_01]: They talk about illusion selectively, denying its existence, because that temporarily, momentarily frees you from fear.

144
00:12:38,397 --> 00:12:47,321
[SPEAKER_01]: But even if there is a mistake, you know, like if you're perceiving something that it's not like that, that experience of a mistake, experience of illusion, is real as such.

145
00:12:48,622 --> 00:12:57,866
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's irrelevant, in a sense, because whether it's real or illusory, the fact that it can make you feel, that's where the problem is.

146
00:12:58,246 --> 00:13:01,208
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling is the problem, not what you've just perceived or not.

147
00:13:05,872 --> 00:13:21,224
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just thinking like that becomes apparent that... Well, thinking like that will make the attitude of denial, saying, ah, the world, the experience, this is not real, this is not real, will make it basically hollow, impotent.

148
00:13:24,846 --> 00:13:26,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's leaking here.

149
00:13:27,749 --> 00:13:33,433
[SPEAKER_01]: It will make it impotent, because it works only if you don't look at the other side of the picture.

150
00:13:34,630 --> 00:13:35,250
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not real.

151
00:13:35,691 --> 00:13:41,513
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the denial that frees you from temporarily... well, seemingly frees you from... Ah!

152
00:13:41,713 --> 00:13:41,813
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah!

153
00:13:42,033 --> 00:13:42,774
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm relieved now.

154
00:13:42,914 --> 00:13:43,774
[SPEAKER_01]: It was actually a rope.

155
00:13:45,455 --> 00:13:49,076
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I said, if you reverse and then don't stop there.

156
00:13:49,096 --> 00:13:50,317
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, deny your rope.

157
00:13:51,097 --> 00:13:52,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Deny your snake by finding out it's a rope.

158
00:13:53,058 --> 00:13:53,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, deny that rope.

159
00:13:54,599 --> 00:13:55,779
[SPEAKER_01]: Deny that piece as well.

160
00:13:55,820 --> 00:13:56,760
[SPEAKER_01]: Why did you stop there?

161
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:01,362
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're only going to deny the displeasure as a form of escape,

162
00:14:01,978 --> 00:14:06,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, why do you... like, you come in good faith, then not deny peace.

163
00:14:06,260 --> 00:14:07,600
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm relieved.

164
00:14:08,161 --> 00:14:09,701
[SPEAKER_01]: But what if it's a snake?

165
00:14:09,941 --> 00:14:17,525
[SPEAKER_01]: And that just shows you that things are subject to change, even if they don't necessarily change.

166
00:14:19,605 --> 00:14:23,427
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize, I cannot control things changing.

167
00:14:23,527 --> 00:14:28,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, the peace I feel now, on account of this particular change, is out of my control too.

168
00:14:29,370 --> 00:14:30,090
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's frightening.

169
00:14:31,220 --> 00:14:37,082
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, at the same time, the suffering that I feel on account of these things right now, is also out of my control.

170
00:14:37,543 --> 00:14:38,423
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's liberating.

171
00:14:40,164 --> 00:14:41,384
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, you want to abandon both.

172
00:14:43,305 --> 00:14:46,667
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to realize that, well, actually, it's irrelevant whether it's...

173
00:14:46,687 --> 00:14:48,007
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it's real or not real.

174
00:14:48,127 --> 00:14:54,490
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact is, it arises as such, and as such, my feelings depend upon it, and as such, I have no control over them.

175
00:14:58,766 --> 00:15:01,589
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why arahant doesn't feel pleasure, nor displeasure.

176
00:15:02,450 --> 00:15:11,438
[SPEAKER_01]: He's not subject to, you know, to like delight and lustful excitement and that form of pleasure, nor he's subject to suffering.

177
00:15:12,459 --> 00:15:22,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Because both are seen as dependent upon things that arise externally, and as such, something that is fundamentally beyond your control.

178
00:15:30,730 --> 00:15:32,230
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing the danger in the result.

179
00:15:32,651 --> 00:15:32,951
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

180
00:15:35,311 --> 00:15:37,972
[SPEAKER_01]: The danger, the gratification and the escape.

181
00:15:47,455 --> 00:15:49,195
[SPEAKER_00]: The gratification being just this.

182
00:15:49,455 --> 00:15:49,695
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry?

183
00:15:49,915 --> 00:15:52,896
[SPEAKER_00]: The gratification being of things arising.

184
00:15:53,296 --> 00:15:54,737
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there is a degree of pleasure.

185
00:15:55,957 --> 00:15:56,197
[SPEAKER_01]: That's...

186
00:15:56,957 --> 00:16:00,098
[SPEAKER_01]: So, gratification is confirmation and delight and rejoicing and

187
00:16:02,944 --> 00:16:12,493
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have to start seeing that pleasure, whether you're gratified or not, as impermanent, as lowly, unworthy, and a trap.

188
00:16:14,235 --> 00:16:16,697
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, like the simile from the suttas,

189
00:16:19,330 --> 00:16:22,932
[SPEAKER_01]: A big cup of beautiful beverage, pleasing in every sense.

190
00:16:23,132 --> 00:16:27,734
[SPEAKER_01]: So, nice smell, nice tartness, nice consistency, nice everything, nice taste.

191
00:16:28,314 --> 00:16:33,737
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, it looks beautiful, but it has poison in it, that you cannot detect, but you know that there is a poison.

192
00:16:34,577 --> 00:16:35,898
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's still not real.

193
00:16:35,918 --> 00:16:36,458
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's real.

194
00:16:36,838 --> 00:16:42,261
[SPEAKER_01]: And the joy of drinking, of experiencing that taste, is also real.

195
00:16:43,948 --> 00:16:46,589
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't know that it has poison, you'll drink it and you'll enjoy it.

196
00:16:47,209 --> 00:16:51,470
[SPEAKER_01]: If you know that it has poison, well, you'll drink it and your mouth will still enjoy it.

197
00:16:52,490 --> 00:16:57,071
[SPEAKER_01]: But you won't enjoy it, because it will be over... overridden?

198
00:16:57,752 --> 00:16:58,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Or overrode?

199
00:17:00,892 --> 00:17:01,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Overrided.

200
00:17:01,973 --> 00:17:02,713
[SPEAKER_01]: Overrided.

201
00:17:02,753 --> 00:17:05,914
[SPEAKER_01]: It will be... Overroded.

202
00:17:06,634 --> 00:17:09,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it will be... it will be overwhelmed by the fear

203
00:17:11,427 --> 00:17:14,168
[SPEAKER_01]: of the knowledge that actually you are drinking poison.

204
00:17:17,109 --> 00:17:17,970
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you won't drink it.

205
00:17:18,230 --> 00:17:28,094
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter how pleasing it is, fully discerning the danger, the poison in it, you will not give in to the gratification of it.

206
00:17:30,575 --> 00:17:33,856
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is no... that's why, like, you you mustn't deny

207
00:17:34,249 --> 00:17:43,452
[SPEAKER_01]: Things, even beautiful and enchanting things, because by denying them, the nature of gratification is being denied, and you're not understanding it, which means you're still trapped by it.

208
00:17:44,732 --> 00:17:46,113
[SPEAKER_01]: So, obviously, it doesn't mean you should indulge.

209
00:17:46,173 --> 00:17:49,134
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, like, no... well, that means go and drink poison.

210
00:17:49,194 --> 00:17:49,374
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

211
00:17:49,994 --> 00:18:02,498
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to refrain from drinking poison, but you have to recognize that the validity of its pleasure still stands on a certain level, on the level of taste, on the level of sight, on the level of smell, touch, ears.

212
00:18:04,985 --> 00:18:06,926
[SPEAKER_01]: On that level, it remains.

213
00:18:08,027 --> 00:18:13,349
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can discern through that, and then the knowledge... Because that knowledge cannot be perceived.

214
00:18:13,429 --> 00:18:14,750
[SPEAKER_01]: Poison is undetectable.

215
00:18:16,291 --> 00:18:23,355
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the whole idea of Krishna escape, of denial, maya, illusion, reality, just doesn't work.

216
00:18:24,235 --> 00:18:29,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there are certain things that can only be known, not perceived.

217
00:18:30,238 --> 00:18:33,620
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can go and see that the rope is a snake, and the snake is a rope, or whatever.

218
00:18:34,786 --> 00:18:38,748
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you can do a scientific experiment and find out that it has traces of poison.

219
00:18:38,928 --> 00:18:41,370
[SPEAKER_01]: But what if it's undetectable by any means?

220
00:18:41,410 --> 00:18:42,811
[SPEAKER_01]: You can approach it through your senses.

221
00:18:44,251 --> 00:18:48,614
[SPEAKER_01]: When the only thing you have is a thought that there is a poison in it.

222
00:18:51,636 --> 00:18:52,876
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why it's so hard to develop.

223
00:18:54,057 --> 00:19:00,601
[SPEAKER_01]: But if it is developed, no amount of pleasure coming from the five senses can actually take you back there.

224
00:19:01,982 --> 00:19:08,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the knowledge is developed on a more fundamental level that precedes the senses in that structural sense.

225
00:19:09,228 --> 00:19:11,990
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you... they just... they're powerless then.

226
00:19:12,730 --> 00:19:24,860
[SPEAKER_01]: If you know that there is a poison, if you calculate that thought enough... Obviously, if there were no poison in sensuality, no matter how much you think that there is a poison, it would not get established.

227
00:19:25,480 --> 00:19:29,063
[SPEAKER_01]: But because in reality there is a poison in sensuality,

228
00:19:30,302 --> 00:19:37,065
[SPEAKER_01]: Thinking that there is a poison will eventually yield the result of you recognizing that poison in your knowledge, which is how the dhamma works.

229
00:19:39,206 --> 00:19:51,611
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is... if the things that the Buddha taught about were not actually already there in those things, you thinking those things will not be able to take root, which means arahanty would not be possible.

230
00:19:52,292 --> 00:19:59,755
[SPEAKER_01]: But because those things are there, thinking according to the way that those things are, will eventually make you recognize that those things are there.

231
00:20:01,146 --> 00:20:05,309
[SPEAKER_01]: But it takes time, which is why the repetition is necessary for practice.

232
00:20:05,550 --> 00:20:09,853
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not you see it once, like you move the curtains and you see what's outside.

233
00:20:09,973 --> 00:20:10,153
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

234
00:20:11,034 --> 00:20:24,225
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to keep doing it, and doing it, and doing it, and doing it, until that almost... that artificial view of looking for poison, which you don't see, you don't detect, nothing, gets so established.

235
00:20:24,705 --> 00:20:28,308
[SPEAKER_01]: And when it's established, then you will actually see the poison, see the danger.

236
00:20:29,935 --> 00:20:37,978
[SPEAKER_01]: And because that danger was truly there, your practice corresponded to it, even without your knowledge, and then it was confirmed, and now you know.

237
00:20:38,978 --> 00:20:40,159
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... well, that was the same.

238
00:20:40,179 --> 00:20:42,860
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... that's like transition from Bhutujan to Sotāpanna.

239
00:20:43,780 --> 00:20:49,262
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't know in the beginning, but he's doing it and doing and looking at things correctly, according to the nature of things.

240
00:20:49,742 --> 00:20:50,363
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't know.

241
00:20:50,723 --> 00:20:55,365
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is that faith as the first step necessary, that he had to take on something.

242
00:20:55,405 --> 00:20:56,345
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't know if it's right,

243
00:20:57,413 --> 00:20:58,234
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the Buddha's teaching.

244
00:20:58,294 --> 00:20:59,835
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he kept doing it, doing it, doing it.

245
00:21:00,175 --> 00:21:06,540
[SPEAKER_01]: And because it was right, because it was how things were, then that becomes apparent eventually.

246
00:21:07,601 --> 00:21:09,402
[SPEAKER_01]: And then it's confirmed from him.

247
00:21:09,582 --> 00:21:11,084
[SPEAKER_01]: So he doesn't need any more faith.

248
00:21:11,644 --> 00:21:15,387
[SPEAKER_01]: Now he sees directly for himself that those things are there.

249
00:21:17,529 --> 00:21:24,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why you have the... sensuality is one of the... one of the types of... one of the assumptions, one of the upādānas.

250
00:21:29,579 --> 00:21:30,660
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly how it works.

251
00:21:30,720 --> 00:21:35,862
[SPEAKER_01]: You just assume something based on not knowing, but then you have no reference to know.

252
00:21:36,582 --> 00:21:39,644
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to accept and trust that there is a poison in sensuality.

253
00:21:41,205 --> 00:21:52,390
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, whenever there is a pleasure, or pull, or enchantment, or delight, you keep, like, pasting the idea of poison over it.

254
00:21:52,410 --> 00:21:56,432
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's artificial, because you're not seeing it.

255
00:21:57,550 --> 00:22:06,713
[SPEAKER_01]: But because it is actually there, and you have faith that it's actually there, it will... it will sort of take its roots, much like when you graft a plant.

256
00:22:06,873 --> 00:22:16,916
[SPEAKER_01]: You bring a completely alien, different plant, different tree, different branch to this tree, and you graft it, and then it takes over, and then emerges and becomes a new tree.

257
00:22:22,390 --> 00:22:29,551
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you actually have to take the view externally, which is why an utterance of another is necessary.

258
00:22:30,811 --> 00:22:32,472
[SPEAKER_01]: And, obviously, knowing how to apply it.

259
00:22:34,872 --> 00:22:36,792
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why we refer to dhamma as the ancient path.

260
00:22:36,832 --> 00:22:38,373
[SPEAKER_01]: It was already there, it just gets obscured.

261
00:22:39,893 --> 00:22:44,894
[SPEAKER_01]: If it weren't, no manu-crafting can take root, because it doesn't correspond to it.

262
00:22:46,174 --> 00:22:51,035
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's necessary, eh... for a Buddha or somebody of that

263
00:22:52,265 --> 00:22:58,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Standing, so to speak, two rounds in the world, because it's not enough to see it once.

264
00:22:58,168 --> 00:23:10,734
[SPEAKER_01]: You might once think that there is a poison, that sensuality is unwanted, but unless you have faith, unless somebody told you that you have to keep seeing that over and over and over again, you won't do it.

265
00:23:12,534 --> 00:23:20,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means the true nature of poison and the danger of sensuality will not be discerned.

266
00:23:21,587 --> 00:23:28,673
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not enough to do it once, which means you can't do the dhamma by accident, or by luck, or by sheer chance.

267
00:23:31,335 --> 00:23:34,658
[SPEAKER_01]: You go, by sheer chance, I realize that there is a poison in centrality.

268
00:23:36,099 --> 00:23:36,299
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

269
00:23:37,080 --> 00:23:38,060
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's undetectable.

270
00:23:39,021 --> 00:23:39,742
[SPEAKER_01]: It's only not...

271
00:23:39,762 --> 00:23:40,843
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, that knowledge.

272
00:23:41,103 --> 00:23:43,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, there might be, because there might be a poison, even if you don't see it.

273
00:23:45,226 --> 00:23:46,867
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the most you're going to get, a possibility.

274
00:23:46,907 --> 00:23:47,428
[SPEAKER_01]: But even then,

275
00:23:49,498 --> 00:23:52,160
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why sometimes a putujana can't free himself from sensuality.

276
00:23:52,921 --> 00:24:01,447
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a possibility of this beautiful, tasty beverage being poisonous, as a simile.

277
00:24:02,648 --> 00:24:04,009
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is always that possibility.

278
00:24:04,689 --> 00:24:10,474
[SPEAKER_01]: But, unless you have a good reason to entertain that possibility and act according to it, you won't do it.

279
00:24:11,334 --> 00:24:13,676
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it just seems unreal, actually.

280
00:24:14,377 --> 00:24:17,319
[SPEAKER_01]: Seems... seems fake, seems artificial, seems imaginary.

281
00:24:17,579 --> 00:24:18,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it is, on that level.

282
00:24:20,464 --> 00:24:26,948
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you keep doing it, it turns out there was a poison and now you're liberated from it.

283
00:24:27,408 --> 00:24:29,329
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can have conclusions free from sensuality.

284
00:24:32,130 --> 00:24:39,034
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the same principle like that when it comes to self-view, but you can't do that by yourself, because it's yourself doing it.

285
00:24:40,235 --> 00:24:47,679
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to take on something completely externally by somebody who already done it, and then keep doing it, and then eventually,

286
00:24:48,570 --> 00:24:52,973
[SPEAKER_01]: It will correspond to the nature of things, which is that nothing can be yourself.

287
00:24:55,595 --> 00:25:00,899
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the same principle of... like, it's undetectable, because your whole experience is about yourself.

288
00:25:02,480 --> 00:25:03,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Someone says, well, it's not self.

289
00:25:03,781 --> 00:25:08,465
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can be, oh, okay, there's no self, there's no self, there's no self, but then that no-no-self is experience of yourself.

290
00:25:12,208 --> 00:25:16,771
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to discern the lack of self in the arisen things.

291
00:25:18,123 --> 00:25:18,943
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't perceive it.

292
00:25:19,003 --> 00:25:19,643
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't see it.

293
00:25:20,484 --> 00:25:21,244
[SPEAKER_01]: You can discern it.

294
00:25:24,105 --> 00:25:27,626
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the mind comes first, and all the senses meet in the mind, and so on.

295
00:25:28,266 --> 00:25:41,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why, again, the common contemporary interpretation of impermanence as a flux, and things changing, and the modern science has proven that nothing is permanent, because the atoms and... That's all based on perception.

296
00:25:42,931 --> 00:25:44,151
[SPEAKER_01]: And as such, it makes no difference.

297
00:25:46,240 --> 00:25:55,442
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I can say this chair is unreal, but if I'm personally attached to that chair, and even if I know it's unreal, you're breaking it, I'll suffer.

298
00:25:55,922 --> 00:25:56,762
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not my attachment.

299
00:25:57,202 --> 00:25:58,442
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter how much you say it's not real.

300
00:26:02,683 --> 00:26:07,904
[SPEAKER_01]: I've seen things externally, like... Because whether it's real or not real, or moving or not, as such, it is real.

301
00:26:08,264 --> 00:26:09,865
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that it has arisen means it's real.

302
00:26:10,285 --> 00:26:11,145
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, that's just delusion.

303
00:26:11,225 --> 00:26:12,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Delusion is real.

304
00:26:24,684 --> 00:26:30,465
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the type of the danger and the escape, the gratification and the escape, is something you cannot perceive through your senses.

305
00:26:31,866 --> 00:26:38,087
[SPEAKER_01]: So, saying that all of the objects are impermanent, ah, this is all changing constantly, that makes no difference.

306
00:26:39,428 --> 00:26:42,088
[SPEAKER_01]: Because as such, as changing constantly, it's real.

307
00:26:43,148 --> 00:26:45,549
[SPEAKER_01]: And as such, you're not free from the nature of death arising.

308
00:27:02,618 --> 00:27:16,568
[SPEAKER_01]: Anicca is basically the impermanence in terms of... Well, a thing is impermanent because it has arisen, not because you witnessed its cessation.

309
00:27:18,430 --> 00:27:22,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever has the nature of arising, for that reason,

310
00:27:23,524 --> 00:27:27,067
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a subject to the nature of ceasing, even if it's still persisting right in front of you.

311
00:27:27,287 --> 00:27:29,728
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's depending on something that I have no control over.

312
00:27:30,329 --> 00:27:34,812
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, if you see... Well, that's something that becomes apparent if you start looking at the manifestation of things.

313
00:27:34,832 --> 00:27:39,996
[SPEAKER_01]: And when I say manifestation, I don't mean like seeing things through your senses or something.

314
00:27:40,016 --> 00:27:44,499
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean them manifesting as phenomena, which is sometimes through your senses, sometimes through your mind.

315
00:27:45,320 --> 00:27:48,642
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even ambiguous, weird, delusional thoughts are real as such.

316
00:27:49,763 --> 00:27:51,464
[SPEAKER_01]: You can designate them, you can talk about them,

317
00:27:52,633 --> 00:27:57,216
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot perceive them by your five senses, but you can perceive them by the mind.

318
00:27:57,596 --> 00:27:58,016
[SPEAKER_01]: They're there.

319
00:28:00,098 --> 00:28:00,838
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's all real.

320
00:28:00,998 --> 00:28:07,122
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to acknowledge that, and then it becomes apparent that whatever has the nature of arising is out of your control.

321
00:28:08,223 --> 00:28:09,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you have no say.

322
00:28:09,644 --> 00:28:16,288
[SPEAKER_01]: It's... well, actually, you have no access to the point of their arising, from where they arise.

323
00:28:16,348 --> 00:28:17,949
[SPEAKER_01]: You just... they just... they're just there.

324
00:28:18,429 --> 00:28:19,810
[SPEAKER_01]: You just find them already there.

325
00:28:20,899 --> 00:28:28,166
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if you're just witnessing something appearing, when that thing appears, it appears as if it's there beforehand.

326
00:28:28,226 --> 00:28:28,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Simultaneous.

327
00:28:29,227 --> 00:28:31,048
[SPEAKER_01]: Not necessarily time involving time.

328
00:28:31,729 --> 00:28:32,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes, this came yesterday.

329
00:28:32,830 --> 00:28:32,970
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

330
00:28:32,990 --> 00:28:35,813
[SPEAKER_01]: Simultaneously appears as something before.

331
00:28:38,295 --> 00:28:47,203
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for that reason, you realize the roots, the foundations of that thing are inconceivable, inaccessible to you.

332
00:28:49,522 --> 00:28:50,783
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what impermanence is.

333
00:28:52,603 --> 00:28:56,906
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if that thing remains standing for the rest of your life, which is actually your body.

334
00:28:58,106 --> 00:29:03,289
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't, like, have your body disappear, and get destroyed, and you still remain standing.

335
00:29:09,752 --> 00:29:15,434
[SPEAKER_00]: And things... things arise because... well, it's possible for them to arise because of consciousness.

336
00:29:16,195 --> 00:29:18,476
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yes, see, but even that...

337
00:29:21,393 --> 00:29:22,393
[SPEAKER_00]: There has to be consciousness.

338
00:29:22,413 --> 00:29:24,173
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't... you can't see that directly.

339
00:29:24,974 --> 00:29:25,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

340
00:29:25,494 --> 00:29:26,454
[SPEAKER_01]: You can explain that.

341
00:29:26,554 --> 00:29:27,854
[SPEAKER_01]: You can say, ah, because of the consciousness.

342
00:29:28,434 --> 00:29:33,235
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you're conceiving that consciousness as the cause of the arising of the things.

343
00:29:33,255 --> 00:29:36,496
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, you're conceiving it as yourself.

344
00:29:36,596 --> 00:29:39,316
[SPEAKER_01]: You're enclosed within the five aggregates and they're manifested.

345
00:29:39,936 --> 00:29:42,417
[SPEAKER_01]: For the reason that they're manifested, they are impermanent.

346
00:29:42,917 --> 00:29:46,417
[SPEAKER_00]: It's enclosed within the five aggregates, as you say.

347
00:29:46,657 --> 00:29:46,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

348
00:29:47,317 --> 00:29:48,478
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't step outside of them.

349
00:29:48,658 --> 00:29:50,818
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't say consciousness is this, consciousness is that.

350
00:29:52,435 --> 00:29:54,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you can't... you can't do that in a good faith.

351
00:29:54,916 --> 00:29:55,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

352
00:29:55,916 --> 00:29:58,737
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can... you can have feelings.

353
00:29:58,997 --> 00:29:59,377
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no.

354
00:29:59,697 --> 00:30:02,798
[SPEAKER_00]: In terms of... you cannot own them.

355
00:30:03,939 --> 00:30:09,581
[SPEAKER_01]: You can just... you can just... Well, yeah, but your description is such appropriate to them.

356
00:30:10,361 --> 00:30:11,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Saying consciousness is the reason for this.

357
00:30:11,921 --> 00:30:13,002
[SPEAKER_01]: You just appropriated it.

358
00:30:14,262 --> 00:30:19,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Someone can't say it arises like this particular thing.

359
00:30:20,340 --> 00:30:24,242
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever it is, by hand, arises within consciousness.

360
00:30:24,883 --> 00:30:27,384
[SPEAKER_00]: Within the self-conscious.

361
00:30:27,525 --> 00:30:27,805
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

362
00:30:28,185 --> 00:30:28,345
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

363
00:30:29,206 --> 00:30:29,386
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

364
00:30:30,727 --> 00:30:35,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that would mean, like, consciousness is some sort of vessel in which things appear, which means you take the ownership of the vessel.

365
00:30:36,630 --> 00:30:38,011
[SPEAKER_01]: It arises with consciousness.

366
00:30:38,291 --> 00:30:42,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, within, your whole experience is within the five aggregates, enclosed.

367
00:30:44,415 --> 00:30:45,076
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you have

368
00:30:45,973 --> 00:30:47,714
[SPEAKER_01]: The experience of my hand has arisen.

369
00:30:48,334 --> 00:30:52,417
[SPEAKER_01]: The experience of having a hand arising in your experience is within already.

370
00:30:53,817 --> 00:30:54,998
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't step outside of it.

371
00:30:55,418 --> 00:31:04,023
[SPEAKER_01]: And saying that arises, oh, inside of the consciousness or inside of this means stepping, means putting it into something, which means you are that which is outside.

372
00:31:04,843 --> 00:31:08,465
[SPEAKER_01]: You become the vessel in which things arise, therefore you become its owner.

373
00:31:08,946 --> 00:31:09,106
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

374
00:31:09,726 --> 00:31:11,427
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever the aggregate is manifested,

375
00:31:15,100 --> 00:31:19,643
[SPEAKER_01]: It's found there beforehand, simultaneously with its manifestation.

376
00:31:22,185 --> 00:31:30,150
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether it's a perception of your hand, or your feeling, or just the knowledge of something, means it's manifested.

377
00:31:31,190 --> 00:31:32,972
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have no say in that manifestation.

378
00:31:35,533 --> 00:31:37,435
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no say, absolutely no say.

379
00:31:40,617 --> 00:31:42,478
[SPEAKER_01]: They arise on their own accord.

380
00:31:44,361 --> 00:31:45,741
[SPEAKER_01]: They persist on their own accord.

381
00:31:47,802 --> 00:31:51,003
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... within that, that's... that's why they're impermanent.

382
00:31:52,543 --> 00:31:59,605
[SPEAKER_01]: So again, witnessing something disappear, means taking an external view in regard to their disappearance.

383
00:32:00,206 --> 00:32:08,288
[SPEAKER_01]: And as such, that disappearance will not pertain to anicca, which is the personal impermanence, the impermanence that frees you from suffering.

384
00:32:11,496 --> 00:32:11,676
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

385
00:32:11,696 --> 00:32:16,199
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you just witnessed it externally, which means that which was witnessing remained unaffected by it.

386
00:32:16,999 --> 00:32:21,301
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you recognize, well, it's not... because cessation of things is something that's manifested.

387
00:32:23,883 --> 00:32:27,605
[SPEAKER_01]: And as such, it's impermanent because it's manifested, not because it's ceasing.

388
00:32:29,386 --> 00:32:34,269
[SPEAKER_01]: So, rising is manifested, cessation is manifested, persisting while changing is manifested.

389
00:32:34,809 --> 00:32:36,350
[SPEAKER_01]: All of those things are impermanent.

390
00:32:36,370 --> 00:32:40,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Impermanence is in regard to all of them, not just bound with cessation.

391
00:32:50,205 --> 00:32:58,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that nature of impermanence is superimposed to any of those particular manifestations of it.

392
00:32:59,127 --> 00:32:59,887
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on a different level.

393
00:32:59,907 --> 00:33:05,648
[SPEAKER_01]: Simultaneously present, inseparable from, but on a different level.

394
00:33:07,868 --> 00:33:16,090
[SPEAKER_01]: It pertains to the nature of things arising, the nature of arising, the nature of ceasing, the nature of persisting while changing.

395
00:33:16,810 --> 00:33:17,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, those three things have come.

396
00:33:18,186 --> 00:33:19,947
[SPEAKER_01]: They have a nature, a nature.

397
00:33:20,767 --> 00:33:26,030
[SPEAKER_01]: That nature is manifested, and as such, it's impermanent, for the reason that it's manifested.

398
00:33:27,391 --> 00:33:28,472
[SPEAKER_01]: If you would...

399
00:33:28,752 --> 00:33:37,336
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were able to be a creator, you would be able to take the possession of cessation, that thing, which means you can have it for as long as you want, but you can't.

400
00:33:38,197 --> 00:33:42,879
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't even conceive, you can't even access the point where things manifest.

401
00:33:42,919 --> 00:33:47,622
[SPEAKER_01]: You just find them as manifested, even if it's simultaneously right now.

402
00:33:49,695 --> 00:33:53,637
[SPEAKER_01]: And as such, when they cease, that's also inaccessible to you.

403
00:33:55,738 --> 00:33:59,879
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why the aggregates cannot be actually assumed in the first place.

404
00:33:59,979 --> 00:34:02,341
[SPEAKER_01]: They cannot be held by upādāna in the first place.

405
00:34:02,641 --> 00:34:03,461
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't take them up.

406
00:34:04,081 --> 00:34:06,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Not knowing that you can't take them up, that's how you take it up.

407
00:34:14,146 --> 00:34:17,367
[SPEAKER_01]: So again, arising is manifested, ceasing is manifested.

408
00:34:17,647 --> 00:34:19,428
[SPEAKER_01]: Resisting while changing is manifested.

409
00:34:20,157 --> 00:34:24,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Manifestation is that which is impermanent, not those particular arising, ceasing, persisting or changing.

